#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065395 :tutorial/content "**Logarithmic differentiation** is a technique to compute derivatives that are difficult to find using the usual rules of differentiation, such as the product, quotient, and chain rules.\n\nLet's consider the following function.\n\n$$\ny = x^x\n$$\n\nWe aim to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$ However, in this case, we note the following:\n\n- Both the base and the exponent are functions of $x.$\n\n- This means we cannot apply the power rule since the exponent is not a fixed number.\n\n- We also cannot apply the rules for differentiating exponentials since the base is not a fixed number.\n\nSo instead, we'll use logarithmic differentiation. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Now, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  y\\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y=x^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^x \\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "21321352-0483-513c-828f-f1c46306015a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6087 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
